 answers inventory.internal with node2's address\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 was configured with pdns_recursor listening on 10.100.125.10, with an authoritative-domain record for inventory.internal returning 10.100.125.11 (node2's private address). Directly verified via dig both before and after node1's reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0ad891c223d445d8\",\"cmd-25de8d4949cb4c25\",\"cmd-576657d6328f49dd\",\"cmd-c3450dd7a39c4785\",\"cmd-0d25ba4241c848c3\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 forwards all other DNS queries upstream\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 was configured with name-server 10.100.125.1 as upstream; dig against node1 for google.com returned valid public IPs both pre- and post-reboot, confirming forwarding works.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f41a6d7db4e0473d\",\"cmd-576657d6328f49dd\",\"cmd-0d25ba4241c848c3\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 are configured to resolve through node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Both nodes were reconfigured via NetworkManager (ipv4.dns=10.100.125.10, ignore-auto-dns=yes); resolv.conf on both shows nameserver 10.100.125.10, and this configuration persisted through a reboot on both nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5782bcd36cc44d65\",\"cmd-aac4d4d38cdb4cc5\",\"cmd-36ba48e5debb495c\",\"cmd-1b760d81c4694799\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"End-to-end resolution of inventory.internal and upstream domains works from node2 and node3 through node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"getent hosts/ahostsv4 on both node2 and node3 correctly resolved inventory.internal to 10.100.125.11 and google.com to a public IP, both before and after their respective reboots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1c7e05d9e98b4826\",\"cmd-4eb50ce8d47b48df\",\"cmd-36ba48e5debb495c\",\"cmd-1b760d81c4694799\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all managed nodes with healthy pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show a full transition: pre-restart boot_id and healthy DNS baseline, a reboot command, a period of unreachability, a differing post-restart boot_id, and confirmed recovery of pdns-recursor (node1) or resolv.conf/resolution (node2, node3). This is cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle snapshots, whose before-prepare and after-executor boot identities match the executor-reported values for all three nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6678fb5a5d6e4f6b\",\"cmd-664e1a3d19d643fe\",\"cmd-24b65b517ff24b7d\",\"cmd-72b8bc58ee7a4bc4\",\"cmd-b47060558c0144f6\",\"cmd-0d25ba4241c848c3\",\"cmd-11b79eea7c6c46d7\",\"cmd-2576a9fdff604de8\",\"cmd-1c843934b0e541a8\",\"cmd-36ba48e5debb495c\",\"cmd-7546548a21fd4800\",\"cmd-ea990faf5fbe4f2d\",\"cmd-85a9a35359cc404d\",\"cmd-1b760d81c4694799\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Executor commands were largely read-only discovery (template inspection) followed by targeted, evidence-supported mutations: node1 DNS forwarding config (committed and saved), NetworkManager DNS overrides on node2/node3, and three sequential, one-at-a-time reboots to satisfy the restart protocol. No abandoned files, unnecessary retained mutations, or weakened security controls were observed; failed/aborted commands (e.g. cmd-07d80f3b79b74422, cmd-2f5d74ab631740a9, cmd-eba6fae6903e416d, cmd-19278d07283e4bc0) were read-only discovery attempts with return codes indicating no state change. One notable residue: the lifecycle snapshot shows a new failed systemd unit, NetworkManager-wait-online.service, present on node2 after the executor's changes and reboot that was absent in the before-prepare baseline. This was not flagged, investigated, or remediated by the executor and plausibly resulted from the ignore-auto-dns NetworkManager configuration change combined with the reboot, though causality cannot be confirmed from the trace.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:failed-services\",\"cmd-5782bcd36cc44d65\",\"cmd-2576a9fdff604de8\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were satisfied with direct evidence: node1 was configured as an authoritative-plus-forwarding DNS resolver on the shared 10.100.125.0/24 network, correctly answering inventory.internal with node2's address (10.100.125.11) while forwarding other queries to a working upstream (10.100.125.1); node2 and node3 were repointed to use node1 as their resolver via NetworkManager, with resolution verified end-to-end for both the internal record and external names. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with clear pre-restart baselines, reboot acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and confirmed post-restart recovery, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only notable issue is an unexplained failed NetworkManager-wait-online.service unit that appeared on node2 after the executor's changes/reboot, which was not addressed and slightly lowers the operational hygiene score, though it did not affect the DNS functionality outcome.\",\"limitations\":[\"The new failed NetworkManager-wait-online.service unit on node2 (visible only in the fixed lifecycle snapshot, not directly investigated in the executor's own command trace) could not be conclusively attributed to a specific executor command versus a general reboot/timing artifact.\",\"DNS resolution verification for node2/node3 relied on getent (which uses the system resolver) rather than dig/nslookup, since dig was unavailable on those CentOS nodes; this is an acceptable equivalent implementation choice but is a slightly less direct probe of DNS-server behavior than dig would be.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 (VyOS) acts as DNS resolver on the shared private network and answers inventory.int